Border Studio's frame, frozen at 597ce7a: what the frame engine is checked against (the parity gate, scripts/parity-borders.mjs: cases 1, 2, 5 and 6, the flat fills; 3 and 4 join with Frosted). CI never fetches the Borders repo; these files are the reference.
refs.json: the six cases and the two photos. Each case is Border Studio's own preview canvas (scale 1, media long side 1800) as a lossless PNG in refs/, its settings (border, fill, blur, corners, ratio, Crop or Fit), where the photo landed (`shown`) and the corner radius, and the same frame in Print Machine's document (`print_machine`: the aspect key and the frame). `width` is the frame's width as Print Machine stores it, the border over the sheet's long side (`width_fraction`: 150 of 2100 on the free sheet, 150 of 1800 on a Fit sheet); `sheet` is the canvas's size. Each photo carries Border Studio's own 84 px sample (`samplePixels`: the pixels with alpha >= 125, one row of the sample a line) and its Photo, Deep, Muted and Comp hex (`buildBorderSuggestions`).
The cases: (1) free ratio, Photo fill; (2) free, corners 100, Deep; (3) free, Frosted Blur 60; (4) free, Frosted Blur 240; (5) Fit 4:5, Muted; (6) Fit 9:16, corners 50, Comp. All on block-land; free is Print Machine's Original sheet, Fit a fixed sheet in Fit. There is no Crop-with-a-ratio case: Border Studio's Crop put the border outside the ratio, which the merge cut, so a comparison there would assert the defect.
The photos (block-land 1800x1200, block-port 1200x1800) are generated by photos.mjs, for capture.mjs and the parity gate alike, not committed: flat colour blocks of unequal area, a gradient band, a flat square at each corner. Border Studio draws them at their own pixels, so the photo's interior compares exactly in the free cases; the parity documents carry the recorded hex, so the gate tests geometry and compositing, and the palette port is tested on the recorded sample.
To capture again: copy the tree (`git -C <checkout> archive 597ce7a | tar -x -C <copy>`, `npm ci` in the copy), then `BORDERS_SRC=<copy> node scripts/parity-borders/capture.mjs`. It refuses a git checkout, checks the copy by hash, patches the copy only (the patch is in its header), builds it, serves it from 127.0.0.1 and drives it in Chromium. The PNGs are deterministic: a second run writes the same bytes.
Border Studio's storage keys appear here and in capture.mjs only (the preset route); the app never reads them, and src/state/borderStudioKeys.test.ts fails if any other source under src/ or scripts/ names one.
